Key teaching points on macro mechanics:
1. `macro_rules!` matches `$iter:ty => $item:ty, impl $($args:tt)*`. The `$($args:tt)*` gobbles all remaining token trees — the comment in the source explains why impl bounds go last (delegate.rs L3-4). Token trees: any token or balanced ()/{} group counts as one tt.
2. `delegate_indexed_iterator!` internally calls `delegate_iterator!` first (L38-41) — macro composition, mirroring the trait subsumption (IndexedParallelIterator: ParallelIterator).
3. The struct must already exist with an `inner` field — convention coupling between macro and struct declaration.
4. `#[macro_use] mod delegate;` in lib.rs (L84-85) — makes the macros textually visible to all modules declared after it; without `#[macro_export]` they're crate-private, so downstream users cannot use them — must hand-write. Also into_par_vec! is defined in collections/mod.rs before the `pub mod` declarations (L10 before L24-30) — macro_rules scoping is textual/order-based.
